{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1315637744/4/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"aaef0977\" lines=\"25-41\">\n<description>\nReplace an orange canvas with concentric Manhattan-distance color rings centered on the lone marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is an orange background with exactly one non-orange marker pixel.\n- The marker color is one of the colors in the fixed non-orange ring cycle.\n\nRule:\n- The marker location is distance 0 and keeps the marker color.\n- Each step of Manhattan distance away from the marker advances one position\n  through the cycle green, yellow, black, grey, red, teal, maroon, pink, blue.\n- Fill every pixel of the original grid by this distance-indexed cycle.\n\nKey insight: The output is not copied from existing objects; it is the\ndistance field around the single marker, colored by a cyclic palette that\nexcludes the orange background.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"898e7135\" lines=\"7-20\">\n<description>\nInflate a frame's hole-layout and fill each hole with its matching piece.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Largest component is a monochrome frame enclosing bg holes.\n- Outside pieces composed of k×k blocks; k = side of smallest solid square piece.\n\nRule:\n- Extract connected hole regions inside the frame as slots.\n- Match each piece to a slot by comparing block-level shapes under rotation/flip.\n- Output the layout scaled by k, with pieces placed in slots; frame color as background.\n\nKey insight: Pieces are k×k-block-scaled versions of holes; downsampling reveals shape matches under all 8 orientations.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9385bd28\" lines=\"71-87\">\n<description>\nFill rectangles between diagonal marker pairs using a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with a vertical legend at the bottom-left: each row is a (marker_color, fill_color) pair.\n- Scattered L-shaped corner markers of each marker color define opposite corners of axis-aligned rectangles.\n- Some markers are solid 2×2 blocks instead of corner pairs.\n\nRule:\n- Read legend bottom-to-top; for each entry find all pixels of that marker color.\n- Determine which two bbox corners carry L-shaped anchors — the *other* diagonal defines the fill region.\n- Fill the diamond (all shortest-path cells between those two corners) with the legend's fill color.\n- Solid 2×2 marker blocks are simply recolored to the fill color.\n- Later legend entries are protected from earlier fills.\n\nKey insight: The L-shaped anchors sit on the marker diagonal; the fill goes on the opposite diagonal of the bounding box.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"edcc2ff0\" lines=\"47-63\">\n<description>\nKeep only the lower-panel colors named by the top markers and expand each marker into an object-count bar.\n\nInput structure\nA black instruction strip sits above a non-black rectangular scene panel.  The strip contains one-cell markers\nin the first column, and each marker color is a candidate object color in the lower panel.  The lower panel has\na unique dominant background color and scattered 4-connected foreground objects.\n\nRule\nIn the lower panel, preserve objects whose colors appear as top markers and recolor every other foreground\nobject to the panel background.  In the instruction strip, replace each marker by a horizontal run from column 0\nwhose length is the number of 4-connected lower-panel objects of that marker color; erase markers with count 0.\n\nKey insight\nThe top strip is a color filter plus a compact count summary: marker colors select which lower-panel objects\nsurvive, and their bars report the number of surviving objects by color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b3084d4\" lines=\"253-266\">\n<description>\nAssemble colored rectangular sprites into a single tiled rectangle, anchored by a marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several solid-colored rectangular sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One sprite has a unique single-pixel marker (different color) at one corner — this is the anchor.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the anchor sprite (contains the marker pixel) and the remaining pieces.\n- Rotate the anchor so the marker pixel lands at position (0,0) of the output.\n- Pack all remaining pieces (allowing 90° rotations) to tile a complete rectangle with no gaps.\n\nKey insight: The lone marker pixel picks the anchor and fixes the output orientation — it must sit at the top-left corner.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"97d7923e\" lines=\"81-94\">\n<description>\nRecolor vertical bars by legend-driven rank selection.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Top-left: monochrome blocks touching row 0; each has color K and height k.\n- Main grid: vertical bars capped top and bottom by a single color, with solid-color interiors.\n\nRule:\n- For each legend entry (K, k), find vertical bars capped by K.\n- Rank bars by interior length (longest = rank 1).\n- Recolor the rank-k bar's interior to K.\n\nKey insight: Legend height is a rank index into length-sorted bars, not a literal size.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"21897d95\" lines=\"283-301\">\n<description>\nRepaint regions from T-shaped control markers, erase the markers, and optionally rotate based on a color-9 L-marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a mosaic of monochrome regions.\n- Color 1 forms T-shaped control markers on region boundaries.\n- Some controls use three singleton color-1 pixels around a colored center; that center is a literal replacement color.\n- An optional 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker triggers 90-degree rotation of the output.\n\nRule:\n- For a color-1 T marker, the single stem points at the destination region to repaint.\n- The two diagonal cells behind the stem identify the source region; the destination gets that source region's original color.\n- For a colored-center T marker, the same stem identifies the destination, but the center color is the replacement.\n- All marker cells are filled with the final color of the source-side region.\n- A 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker controls rotation: ┐ (corner top-right) → CCW 90°, ┌ (corner top-left) → CW 90°.\n\nKey insight:\n- The stem chooses what changes, while the two diagonals behind the stem choose where the marker itself belongs.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"45a5af55\" lines=\"5-18\">\n<description>\nBuild nested square frames from a stack of horizontal color stripes.\n\nInput structure:\n- Full-width horizontal stripes of distinct non-bg colors, stacked vertically.\n- Each stripe has uniform color and variable height (1-4 rows).\n\nRule:\n- Read stripes bottom to top.\n- Bottom stripe becomes the center solid square with side length = stripe height.\n- Each subsequent stripe wraps the current shape with a square frame of thickness = stripe height, in that stripe's color.\n\nKey insight: Stripe height doubles as both frame thickness and (for the innermost) center-square side length; output dimensions are fully determined by the stripe heights.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"aaecdb9a\" lines=\"10-26\">\n<description>\nConvert scattered foreground objects into bottom-aligned count bars in a fixed five-color order.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color and foreground objects in the task\npalette: grey, red, teal, maroon, and pink. Objects are monochromatic\n8-connected components; some palette colors may be absent.\n\nRule\nFor each palette color in order grey, red, teal, maroon, pink, count its\n8-connected objects. Create a background grid with five columns and height\nequal to the largest count, then fill each column upward from the bottom\nwith its palette color for that count.\n\nKey insight\nThe output does not preserve object shapes or positions; it is a histogram\nof per-color 8-connected object counts, using the learned palette order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"62593bfd\" lines=\"32-46\">\n<description>\nSort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).\n- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).\n- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).\n- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.\n\nKey insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"83eb0a57\" lines=\"72-86\">\n<description>\nCrop the largest object and overlay each smaller sprite where its shared marker pattern matches.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the most common color, and the foreground has one largest destination object plus smaller source sprites.\n- Each source sprite shares exactly one marker color with the destination object.\n\nRule\n- Output the tight bbox crop of the largest object.\n- Translate each source sprite so every connected marker component of the shared color exactly matches destination marker components.\n- Paste source sprites in decreasing area order, so smaller sprites can sit on top of larger ones when they overlap.\n\nKey insight\n- The shared-color marker components are alignment keys; matching whole marker components distinguishes singleton slots from larger same-color marker blocks.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7c66cb00\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nDock two-tone sprites into color-matched horizontal bars with a color swap.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
